  "worked_solution": "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4y & =5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}-4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2x)-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=8$ and $y=11,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2x)-8\\cdot 4 & =0 \\\\ 2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-32 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $x,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $y=11$ into the equation of the parabola.  \n\nAt the point where $y=11,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4y & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4(11) & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-44 & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =49 \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $x>0$ since we're only interested in the first quadrant. \n\nFinally, substituting $x=7,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-32 & =0 \\\\ 2(7)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-32 & =0 \\\\ 14\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =32 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{32}{14} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{16}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
